{"contract_version":"akw-js-runtime-v1","ts":"2026-08-20T02:32:10.473Z","type":"workflow.log","phase":"Report","message":"vliw-bundle-packing-optimization: VLIW bundle-packing + SIMD vectorization optimization\nBaseline: 147734 cycles\nCorrectness: PASSED\nMeasured cycles: 6896\nSpeedup: 21.4231x\nEvaluation directory: /Users/haiyan-infiniai/Agent4Kernel/vliw-dsh-kersor-10/.kersor/20260817-175215/workflow-authoring/candidates/candidate-1\nCandidate SHA-256: 78993c2963b0ad838a858add2ea056b7294e425b6807f963cad7a91bac6c3b11\nOptimization summary: This implementation adds dependency-aware VLIW packing with per-engine capacity checks and end-of-cycle hazard protection. It vectorizes contiguous index/value loads, hash stages, parity/child selection, bounds wrapping, and stores across VLEN=8 lanes. The unavoidable irregular forest lookup remains eight scalar address/load operations per vector group, but those operations are bundled across groups and then consumed as a contiguous vector scratch range.\nBundling strategy: build(..., vliw=True) greedily packs the already-topologically-ordered slot stream. A candidate joins the current bundle only when its engine has capacity and its scratch reads/writes have no RAW, WAR, or WAW overlap with slots already in that bundle. Debug slots are barriers so correctness observers see committed values. The generated stream is phase-ordered across all vector groups: address formation, vector input loads, scalar forest gathers, vector hash stages, vector control arithmetic, and paired vector stores.\nVectorization notes: The forest array is indexed by data-dependent per-lane indices, and the ISA exposes no vector gather, so it is intentionally not vloaded. Each group computes eight scalar forest addresses and loads eight scalar node values into one contiguous scratch range. That range is then used by VALU XOR/hash operations. Scratch is reused across rounds and sized per group; for batch_size=256 it remains within the 1536-word scratch limit. The code assumes batch_size is VLEN-aligned, matching the benchmark and the architecture's contiguous vload/vstore contract."}
